{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "02-free-bound.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Free and bound variables",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Tell the variables a λ owns from the ones it does not, and see when renaming one is harmless.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What does $\\lambda x.\\, x\\ y$ do? Given $a$ it gives $a\\ y$. The $x$ is a placeholder for the argument. The $y$ is something else: a value from outside the function, which this term does not fix. Mathematics has the same two roles: in $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} i\\,k$ and $\\int_0^1 x\\,t\\ dx$, the $i$ and the $x$ are placeholders, and $n$, $k$ and $t$ come from outside.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> $\\sum_{j=1}^{n} j\\,k$ means the same as $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} i\\,k$; renaming $k$ changes the meaning. In $\\lambda x.\\, x\\ y$, which of $x$ and $y$ could you rename without changing what the function does?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Bound and free",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The answer is $x$: the λ owns it, and renaming it together with the λ changes nothing. The $y$ belongs to whoever supplies it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Free and bound\n> In $\\lambda x.\\, M$ the λ **binds** $x$: every $x$ in $M$ (not under another $\\lambda x$) refers to it. An occurrence of a variable is **bound** when some λ above it has its name, and **free** otherwise. The free variables:\n> $$FV(x) = \\{x\\}, \\quad FV(\\lambda x.\\, M) = FV(M) \\setminus \\{x\\}, \\quad FV(M\\ N) = FV(M) \\cup FV(N).$$\n> A term with no free variables is **closed**, a **combinator**.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Each clause says one thing: a variable alone is free; a λ takes its own variable out; an application has the free variables of both sides. The command `fv:` computes them and names the bound ones:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fv: λx. x y",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same name can be free in one place and bound in another. Here $x$ is bound on the left and free on the right, and $y$ the other way round, so both are listed as free and as bound:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fv: (λx. x y) (λy. x y)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fv: λf. λx. f (f x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Renaming placeholders",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So $\\lambda x.\\, x$ and $\\lambda y.\\, y$ are the same function: both give back what they get. Renaming a bound variable, everywhere it is bound, changes nothing. Now try it on $\\lambda x.\\, y$, the function that ignores its argument and gives $y$. Rename $x$ to $y$ and you get $\\lambda y.\\, y$, the identity: the new name caught the free $y$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] α-equivalence\n> Renaming a bound variable, together with every occurrence it binds, to a name not free in its body gives the same function. Terms that differ only so are **α-equivalent**, and are treated as equal: $\\lambda x.\\, x$ and $\\lambda y.\\, y$ are, $\\lambda x.\\, y$ and $\\lambda y.\\, y$ are not.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`alpha: s, t` decides it, by comparing the terms with their bound names removed (lesson 6 shows how):",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "alpha: λx. λy. x y, λa. λb. a b",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "alpha: λx. y, λy. y",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Keep the failure in mind: lesson 3 meets it again, in the middle of a β-step.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Free variables follow the definition clause by clause.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- The free variables: FV(x) = {x}, FV(λx. b) = FV(b) \\ {x}, FV(f a) = FV(f) ∪ FV(a). -/\ndef freeVars : Term → List String\n  | var x => [x]\n  | lam x b => (freeVars b).filter (· != x)\n  | app f a => freeVars f ++ freeVars a\n\n#eval freeVars (lam \"x\" (app (var \"x\") (var \"y\")))\n#eval freeVars K\n\n/-- A term is closed (a combinator) when it has no free variables. -/\ndef closed (t : Term) : Bool := (freeVars t).isEmpty\n\nexample : closed K = true := by decide\nexample : closed (lam \"x\" (var \"y\")) = false := by decide",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem freeVars_lam_self (x : String) (b : Term) : x ∉ freeVars (lam x b) := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "A λ binds its own variable: $x$ is never free in $\\lambda x.\\, b$.",
      "leanSolution": "  simp [freeVars]",
      "hints": [
        "Unfold `freeVars`: what does `filter (· != x)` leave out?",
        "`simp [freeVars]` does it."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Answer `fv` questions with a set, like `{a, b}` (`{}` for none), and `alpha` questions with `true` or `false`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fv: λx. x y (λy. y z)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Which variables are free in $\\lambda x.\\, x\\ y\\ (\\lambda y.\\, y\\ z)$?",
      "hints": [
        "The $y$ inside $\\lambda y$ is bound there; the first $y$ is not under it."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fv: (λx. λy. x) y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Which variables are free in $(\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, x)\\ y$?",
      "hints": [
        "The last $y$ is the argument, outside both λs."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "alpha: λx. λy. y x, λy. λx. x y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Are $\\lambda x.\\, \\lambda y.\\, y\\ x$ and $\\lambda y.\\, \\lambda x.\\, x\\ y$ α-equivalent?",
      "hints": [
        "Both take two arguments and apply the second to the first."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "alpha: λx. x y, λy. y y",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Are $\\lambda x.\\, x\\ y$ and $\\lambda y.\\, y\\ y$ α-equivalent?",
      "hints": [
        "Renaming $x$ to $y$ would capture the free $y$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A λ binds its variable in its body; every other variable is free, a value from outside. Bound names can be changed at will, as long as no free variable gets captured. That is α-equivalence, and terms are equal up to it.\n\nA β-step puts an argument into a body. What if the argument has a free $y$ and the body has a $\\lambda y$? Lesson 3.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
